The passive provider-health record in src/provider-health.ts is adapted from that project's
crates/ai-memory-llm/src/health.rs, whose module documentation states the rule this file
holds to: "These wrappers never probe providers on their own. They only record the result
of calls the server was already going to make." Taken with it: the status ladder, the
last-call / last-success / last-error triple, error-message truncation at a named constant,
the rule that a success clears the previous error rather than leaving a stale one beside it,
and the snapshot-as-wire-format split. Upstream's provider-role decorator becomes a
per-target record here, because a route chain is a list of targets rather than a pair of
roles.

Two prompt blocks in src/preservation-prompt.ts are adapted from the same commit's
crates/ai-memory-consolidate/prompts/batch_consolidate_system.md: SECURITY_BOUNDARY_BLOCK
from :6-19 (`## SECURITY BOUNDARY`) and FAITHFULNESS_BLOCK from :20-60
(`## FAITHFULNESS -- the most important rule`). Taken: the section headings; the recency
rule that later material supersedes earlier and that a superseded draft may appear as
history but never as current fact; the concrete "Do NOT" list (no invented dates,
versions, commit hashes, paths, function names, line numbers or error codes; no
"When to use"/"Gotchas"/"Best practices"/"Alternative approaches" reference-material
sections; no alternatives never considered; no expanding terse user comments into essays;
no fabricated code examples; no speculation absent from the source); and the framing that
conversation history is untrusted DATA which cannot supply facts, authorize disclosure,
request tool use, or override the output contract. Adapted: upstream consolidates a
session into wiki pages, so its wording is about pages, `kind` classification and
wikilinks; ours is about a compaction summary. Upstream's untrusted input is observations
plus a project preference file; ours is the conversation history plus the operator's own
customInstructions, which is why this project adds its own OPERATOR FOCUS heading and
frames that text as advisory.

Upstream's daemon substrate, embedding provider, marker-file walk, prompt-preference file
and eval harness are not used.

This project also adapts portions of pi-observational-memory:
https://github.com/elpapi42/pi-observational-memory
Copyright (c) 2026 pi-observational-memory contributors, licensed under the MIT License.
Read at commit 497fcfbff1c240f020216b574a26932d23ab10fc (repo main, 2026-07-29) -- a clone
of the repository at that commit, NEVER npm:pi-observational-memory@3.0.3. That distinction
is load-bearing rather than ceremonial: the published tarball declares the SAME version
string as this commit while lacking the observer chunk cap, the oversized-entry handling,
the stream-error logging and the pi-0.81 stream-function fix, because eight src/ commits
shipped under one unchanged version. The drift is invisible to semver, so only a commit
identifies the code.

src/preservation.ts and src/observer.ts adapt these mechanisms, by that project's own
stealList numbering as recorded in docs/research/compaction-v2/dive-pi-observational-memory.md:

  - Coverage-ranked forgetting (steal 1), from agents/dropper/coverage.ts:12-31: the
    none/partial/strong tiers, and the INVERTED drop rank in which `strong` is cheapest to
    drop and `none` dearest. The inversion is upstream's insight and is kept exactly.
    Retargeted from upstream's supportingObservationIds relation to a `supersedes` relation,
    because this project has one record type where upstream has observations and reflections.
  - The deterministic fold at the compaction boundary (steal 2), from
    session-ledger/projection.ts:173-208 and render-summary.ts:20-31, including the
    `[id] timestamp [relevance] content` line format and the recall-teaching preamble with
    its bound ("Do not use recall as broad search or inject raw source unless it is
    needed", render-summary.ts:10). ONE FUNDAMENTAL DIVERGENCE: upstream returns its
    rendered fold AS the compaction summary and makes zero model calls, which is the
    inverted thesis this project declines. Here the fold is rendered into pi's own
    compact() as customInstructions; pi still writes the summary, the entry stays native,
    and fileOps and fromHook semantics are unchanged.
  - The recall back-channel (steal 3), from tools/recall-observation.ts and
    session-ledger/recall.ts: id-to-verbatim-source exchange, with upstream's collision
    handling, its active-vs-dropped status, and its source_unavailable outcome all kept,
    because those three are what make the answer honest. Registered as `recall-fact`, not
    upstream's `recall`, since tool names are first-registration-wins and a bare `recall`
    would collide.
  - Code-enforced source-id citation (steal 4), from agents/observer/agent.ts:69-84 and
    106-110 (normalizeSourceEntryIds and its use inside the tool's own execute):
    upstream's allowlist semantics unchanged, including that ANY id outside the allowlist
    rejects the whole record, that an empty list rejects, and that surviving ids are
    de-duplicated and sorted into the allowlist's order.
  - `ratio` threshold mode (steal 6), from config.ts:80-85 (resolveCompactAfterTokens),
    retargeted: upstream expresses its proactive COMPACTION threshold as a fraction of the
    active model's context window, while this project applies the same shape to the
    OBSERVATION threshold, because this package never decides when to compact.
  - The deferred-and-re-checked trigger (steal 7), from hooks/compaction-trigger.ts:55-74:
    setTimeout(0), then re-assert ctx.isIdle() before doing the work, with the ctx fields
    captured synchronously for the reason upstream's own comment gives.
  - The compaction re-entrancy guard (steal 8), from hooks/compaction-hook.ts:17-25, MINUS
    its `{cancel: true}` return. Cancelling a compaction ends a session, so a duplicate here
    declines only this package's extra work and lets pi's compaction proceed.
  - The observerChunkMaxTokens derived cap (steal 10), from config.ts:104-129
    (resolveObserverChunkMaxTokens, added upstream by commit 2057c10 and present only on
    main). Upstream's constants are kept -- OBSERVER_CHUNK_CONTEXT_RATIO 0.2, the 60000
    fallback, the 256 floor -- as is its rationale, which is why the cap is not optional:
    an uncapped backlog that outgrows the model's window makes every observer call fail, so
    coverage never advances and the session can never recover. Upstream's head/tail excerpt
    for a single oversized entry (commit d0ebd32) is simplified to a marked clip of that one
    entry, keeping the property that matters.
  - The source-addressed chunk format from src/serialize.ts:199-236
    (serializeSourceAddressedBranchEntries), including the `[Source entry id: <id>]` label
    that makes the citation allowlist legible to the model, and its oldest-first drain.
  - hashId (src/ids.ts, sha256 truncated to 12 hex chars), estimateStringTokens and
    estimateEntryTokens (src/tokens.ts), and MAX_RECORD_CONTENT_CHARS (src/serialize.ts:102).

Not taken from that project: its agentLoop-driven observer/reflector/dropper pipeline and
typebox tool schemas (which would make @earendil-works/pi-agent-core a dependency of this
package for one background call, against the zero-runtime-deps rule this package keeps);
its om:status / om:view commands; its PI_OBSERVATIONAL_MEMORY_PASSIVE env key; its
process-lifetime config cache; and -- emphatically -- its default of resolving background
workers to the SESSION model with no cooldown, queue or rate limit, which is the mechanism
behind the one substantiated community report of background observation starving local
inference. The worker here resolves only through this package's own route table and
cooldown store, and never borrows the session model or the compaction chain.

